.\" SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 lumtail contributors
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt LUMTAIL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm lumtail
.Nd read, follow, filter and export log records
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar file ...
.Nm
.Fl -follow
.Op Ar options
.Ar file
.Nm
.Fl -list-modules
.Op Fl -rules Ar file
.Nm
.Fl -sandbox-status
.Op Fl -require-sandbox
.Nm
.Fl -cli-contract | -agent-schema | -record-schema
.Nm
.Fl -help | -help-all | -version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
helps system administrators read logs, select records and export them with
their source locations.
It recognizes formats separately for each record, so one file can contain
several formats.
Unrecognized records use the
.Cm plain
format and remain visible.
.Pp
Start with one of these commands:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail application.log
lumtail --errors-only application.log
lumtail --follow --errors-only application.log
command | lumtail
.Ed
.Pp
The first command displays every record.
The second selects ERROR and CRITICAL records; the third also waits for new
records, starting at the beginning of the file.
The last command reads another program's standard output.
.Pp
Use
.Fl -output Cm json
to export all parsed fields and original record content, or
.Fl -agent
for bounded JSON output suited to automated queries.
Filters work with every output mode.
.Pp
Records go to standard output.
Diagnostics go to standard error and the exit status reports success or failure.
Files and records keep input order, including with parallel parsing.
.Sh OPERANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ar
.It Ar file
Read this input path.
Multiple paths are read in argument order; physical line numbers restart at 1
for each path.
.It Fl
Read standard input at this position in the file list.
With operands omitted,
.Nm
reads standard input.
.El
.Pp
An invocation with no arguments and both standard input and standard output
connected to terminals prints a short guide.
Use
.Ql lumtail -
to enter records directly at a terminal; end input with Ctrl-D.
.Pp
Options accept
.Ql --option value
and
.Ql --option=value .
Use a separate argument for each short option, for example
.Ql -j 4 .
The
.Fl -
argument ends option parsing, allowing filenames beginning with a hyphen.
Prefix a file literally named
.Ql -
with its directory, for example
.Pa ./- .
Numeric arguments are positive decimal integers representable on the host;
narrower ranges are given below.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss Input and record assembly
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl f , Fl -follow
Read exactly one named regular file from its beginning, then wait for new
complete lines.
Follow rename/recreate rotation and observed truncation.
See
.Sx FOLLOWING FILES .
.It Fl -multiline Cm off | indent
Choose physical lines or indentation-based logical records.
The default is
.Cm off .
With
.Cm indent ,
space- or tab-prefixed lines attach to the preceding header.
See
.Sx MULTILINE RECORDS .
.It Fl -multiline-timeout-ms Ar milliseconds
Flush a pending indented record after this idle interval on live input.
The range is 1 through 60000; the default is 200.
.It Fl -max-line-bytes Ar bytes
Set the physical line limit; the default is 1048576 (1 MiB).
The count excludes LF and includes a preceding CR.
An oversized line stops the current input with status 1.
.It Fl -max-record-bytes Ar bytes
Set the assembled multiline record limit, including internal newlines.
The default is 1048576 (1 MiB).
An oversized assembled record fails with status 1.
.It Fl -line-buffered
Emit available completed logical records without waiting for an input batch.
Use this for interactive pipelines.
.El
.Ss Selection
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -errors-only , Fl -high-severity
Select ERROR and CRITICAL records.
.It Fl -min-level Ar level
Select the named severity and higher, excluding UNKNOWN.
Severity names and aliases are case-insensitive; see
.Sx SEVERITY .
.It Fl -where Ar field=value
Require an exact case-sensitive field value.
Repeat this option to require every condition.
Split at the first equals sign; an empty value matches a present empty field.
Missing fields fail the comparison.
For example,
.Ql --where level=ERROR
matches normalized severity, and
.Ql --where service=api
selects one service.
.It Fl -filter Ar expression
Require a Boolean filter expression.
Repeated expressions and all other selection options combine with AND.
See
.Sx FILTER EXPRESSIONS
for the grammar and numeric comparisons.
.It Fl -alerts-only
Require
.Ql event_type=alert .
.It Fl -auth-failures
Require both
.Ql event_type=authentication
and
.Ql outcome=failure .
.El
.Pp
Filters inspect complete values before agent clipping and keep or discard an
entire logical record.
The shortcuts use exact fields; for example, combining
.Fl -alerts-only
with
.Fl -auth-failures
selects zero records because the event types differ.
.Ss Output and display
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -output Cm text | raw | json | agent
Choose the output representation.
The default is
.Cm text .
See
.Sx OUTPUT .
.It Fl -template Ar text
Use an explicit text layout, for example
.Ql '{file}:{line} [{level}] {message}' .
This overrides any preset regardless of argument order.
Fields use braces; missing fields display a hyphen and present empty fields
stay empty.
Use doubled braces for literal braces.
See
.Xr lumtail-rules 5
for template syntax.
.It Fl -preset Cm security | network | auth
Choose a text layout:
.Bl -tag -width security -compact
.It Cm security
.Li {timestamp} [{level}] {source} | {message}
.It Cm network
.Li {timestamp} [{level}] {src_ip} \[u2192] {dst_ip} | {message}
.It Cm auth
.Li {timestamp} [{level}] {source} user={user} remote={src_ip} outcome={outcome} | {message}
.El
.It Fl -color Cm auto | always | never
Control severity coloring in text output.
The default,
.Cm auto ,
uses color on a terminal unless
.Ev NO_COLOR
is present or
.Ev TERM
equals
.Cm dumb .
The
.Cm always
and
.Cm never
settings override automatic selection.
Only the first displayed severity placeholder is highlighted.
.El
.Ss Formats and rules
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -list-modules
List modules in detection order and exit after loading any supplied rules.
Terminal output includes descriptions; redirected output is one name, tab and
decimal priority per line.
.It Fl -rules Ar file
Load a version 1 JSON rules file.
Repeat to load several files in argument order.
There is no automatically loaded rules file.
See
.Xr lumtail-rules 5 .
.It Fl -module Ar name
Force one registered parser.
Unmatched records use
.Cm plain ;
an unknown name is a configuration error with status 2.
.El
.Ss Bounded queries and agent output
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -agent
Select
.Fl -output Cm agent .
An explicit different output mode conflicts with this flag.
.It Fl -agent-fields Ar field,...
Add selected normalized fields to agent records.
Accept up to 16 unique names; the last occurrence replaces earlier selections.
Requires agent output.
.It Fl -limit Ar count
Stop after this many matching logical records, globally across all input files.
The default is 100 with agent output and unlimited with other modes.
.It Fl -duration-ms Ar milliseconds
Stop input collection after 1 through 86400000 milliseconds.
The timer starts after worker initialization.
Use this when following a file or reading a pipe that stays open.
.It Fl -max-message-bytes Ar bytes
Cap encoded JSON string content in agent output.
The default is 2048.
Selected field values share one additional budget of this size.
Requires agent output; see
.Xr lumtail-agent 7
for exact accounting.
.It Fl -max-lines-per-record Ar count
Cap decoded lines in the agent message and each selected field value.
The default is 20.
Requires agent output.
.It Fl -fail-on-truncation
Fail with status 1 before writing an agent record containing any clipped string.
The diagnostic identifies its file and first physical line.
Complete records written before it stay on standard output.
Requires agent output.
.El
.Ss Pipeline assertions
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -expect-none
Require zero matching logical records; return status 3 if a match is observed.
.It Fl -expect-some
Require at least one matching logical record; return status 3 if none is
observed.
.El
.Pp
These flags keep record output unchanged and are mutually exclusive.
Repeating the same flag is accepted.
I/O and processing errors take precedence over an assertion result.
If downstream closes before any known match, either assertion returns 1 as
inconclusive; a known match satisfies
.Fl -expect-some
and fails
.Fl -expect-none .
.Ss Workers and statistics
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl j Ar count , Fl -threads Ar count
Use 1 through 256 parsing workers, or
.Cm auto
for automatic selection (the default).
Automatic selection uses available CPUs, capped at 32.
Linux and
.Fx
also use the affinity mask; Linux additionally uses cgroup v2
.Pa cpu.max ,
rounding a fractional CPU quota up.
One worker runs synchronously.
Unfiltered raw output bypasses parsing and the worker pool.
.It Fl -stats
Write one final throughput and resource line to standard error after processing.
Text and raw modes use English text; JSON and agent modes use a JSON object.
See
.Sx STATISTICS .
.It Fl -stats-version Ar version
Select the statistics line version, 1 or 2, and enable
.Fl -stats .
Version 2 adds the stop reason.
The default is 1.
.El
.Ss Sandbox and interface discovery
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -sandbox-status
Report the active sandbox and exit.
Combine with
.Fl -require-sandbox
to test whether the required native sandbox is available.
.It Fl -require-sandbox
Require the native production sandbox before loading rules or reading logs.
An unavailable required sandbox returns 1.
A production build satisfies it with Landlock and seccomp on Linux,
.Xr unveil 2
and
.Xr pledge 2
on
.Ox ,
or Capsicum on
.Fx .
Run
.Ql lumtail --sandbox-status --require-sandbox
to check a host before deployment.
Deployment details are in
.Pa share/doc/lumtail/docs/runtime-security.md .
.It Fl -api-version Ar version
Select CLI API 1, currently the only accepted value.
Omitting this option also selects API 1.
The CLI API version is independent of the executable release version.
.It Fl -cli-contract
Print the published CLI API 1 definition as JSON and exit.
.It Fl -agent-schema
Print the agent record JSON Schema and exit.
.It Fl -record-schema
Print the full JSON record schema, including multiline continuation, and exit.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the short guide and exit.
.It Fl -help-all
Print the extended guide and exit.
.It Fl -version
Print the executable version and exit.
.El
.Pp
Help, version and schema queries read neither logs nor rules.
For options selecting a single value, the last occurrence wins.
Rules append, filters combine with AND, and the explicit template takes
precedence over presets.
.Sh SEVERITY
Ordered levels are TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR and CRITICAL.
UNKNOWN denotes an absent or unmapped severity and is excluded by severity
thresholds and error shortcuts.
Accepted aliases are:
.Bl -column CRITICAL "Aliases" -offset indent
.It Sy Level Ta Sy Aliases
.It TRACE Ta trace, 10
.It DEBUG Ta debug, 20
.It INFO Ta info, information, notice, 30
.It WARN Ta w, warn, warning, 40
.It ERROR Ta err, error, 50
.It CRITICAL Ta fatal, critical, crit, alert, emerg, emergency, 60
.El
.Pp
Numeric aliases follow Pino/Bunyan levels.
Producer-specific mappings, such as syslog PRI and CEF severity, are described
in
.Xr lumtail-formats 7 .
.Sh FILTER EXPRESSIONS
Quote the complete expression in the shell:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --filter 'level=error AND service=api' app.log
lumtail --filter 'status>=500 AND status<600' access.log
lumtail --filter 'user="Alice Smith" OR src_ip~192.168.*' app.log
.Ed
.Pp
Operators are:
.Bl -tag -width "field>=number" -offset indent
.It Ar field Ns = Ns Ar value
Exact equality.
.It Ar field Ns != Ns Ar value
A present field with a different value.
.It Ar field Ns ~ Ns Ar prefix Ns *
Prefix comparison, with exactly one trailing asterisk.
.It Ar field Ns < Ns Ar number , Ar field Ns <= Ns Ar number
Numeric upper bound, exclusive or inclusive.
.It Ar field Ns > Ns Ar number , Ar field Ns >= Ns Ar number
Numeric lower bound, exclusive or inclusive.
.It Cm NOT Ar expression
Boolean negation.
.It Ar expression Cm AND Ar expression
Both conditions must match.
.It Ar expression Cm OR Ar expression
Either condition must match.
.It Pq Ar expression
Group an expression.
.El
.Pp
Precedence is NOT, then AND, then OR.
Boolean keywords are case-insensitive.
Field names use ASCII letters, digits,
.Ql _ ,
.Ql . ,
.Ql - ,
.Ql @
and
.Ql % .
Use JSON double-quoted strings for empty values, whitespace, parentheses and
escaped characters.
Equality treats an asterisk literally.
.Pp
Missing fields fail every comparison, including inequality.
Thus
.Ql NOT missing=x
is true when the field is absent.
Equality and inequality on
.Cm level
accept normalized severity aliases; other values are case-sensitive.
In contrast,
.Fl -where
always compares the exact normalized string.
.Pp
The names
.Cm file , line , format
and
.Cm raw
address record metadata.
Other names address literal normalized field keys.
A dot is an ordinary key character:
.Ql http.status
names the key
.Cm http.status .
Use full JSON output to discover the keys in a record.
.Pp
Numeric comparisons use exact decimal values, including integers beyond 2^53.
Operands follow JSON number syntax, with at most 128 decoded bytes and an
explicit exponent between -1000000 and 1000000.
Fractions and exponents are accepted; leading plus signs, leading integer
zeros, surrounding whitespace inside a value, units, NaN and infinities fail
numeric validation.
Invalid thresholds cause status 2 before input is opened.
Missing or invalid record values fail numeric comparisons; NOT then applies
normally.
.Pp
String equality distinguishes
.Ql 1 ,
.Ql 1.0
and
.Ql 1e0 ;
numeric comparisons treat them as equal.
Use
.Fl -min-level
for severity ordering.
Each filter expression is limited to 16384 bytes, 128 nodes and 32 nesting
levels.
.Sh OUTPUT
.Bl -tag -width agent
.It Cm text
Render the module template, preset or explicit template.
Terminal controls are escaped while ordinary Unicode is kept.
Continuation lines appear below the formatted header.
.It Cm raw
Write the complete original record after CRLF normalization, adding a newline
even to an unterminated final record.
Raw output writes the record bytes directly; use it to export selected input.
.It Cm json
Write one compact JSON object per line with
.Cm format , level , fields , raw , file
and
.Cm line .
The
.Cm fields
object contains string values; nested JSON objects and arrays become compact
JSON strings.
Multiline records add a
.Cm continuation
string.
Invalid UTF-8 becomes U+FFFD; control characters, direction controls and
Unicode line separators are escaped.
.It Cm agent
Write compact JSON Lines containing timestamp, severity, message and source
location, with optional selected fields and explicit clipping markers.
See
.Xr lumtail-agent 7
for the complete schema and byte budgets.
.El
.Pp
JSON and agent modes emit JSON without ANSI coloring.
The source
.Cm line
is the first physical line, counted from 1; standard input uses
.Ql -
as its
.Cm file .
Read object keys by name when scripting.
API 1 keeps the meaning of every documented field and lets later releases add
fields and formats.
.Sh MULTILINE RECORDS
With
.Fl -multiline Cm indent ,
a nonindented or empty line starts a record.
Following lines starting with a space or tab join it; an indented first line
becomes its own header.
The header selects the parser and supplies fields and severity.
.Pp
Assembly resets at file boundaries, observed truncation and rotation.
Normal EOF flushes the pending record immediately.
On live input the idle timeout flushes it; a later continuation starts a new
record.
On an input error, completed records survive and the unfinished record is
discarded.
.Pp
For JSON output,
.Cm fields.message
is the header message,
.Cm continuation
contains the attached lines, and
.Cm raw
contains the complete normalized record.
Agent output combines the header message and continuations before clipping.
.Sh FOLLOWING FILES
Follow mode polls every 100 milliseconds.
After rename/recreate rotation,
.Nm
opens and validates the replacement, drains the old descriptor to its next
EOF, discards any remaining partial line and switches to the new file.
Physical line numbering restarts at 1.
Observed truncation also restarts reading and assembly.
.Pp
Use
.Fl -duration-ms
for a bounded monitoring interval.
A matching-record limit alone waits until enough records match.
At a deadline, an unfinished physical line is discarded and complete assembled
and queued records finish processing.
.Sh STATISTICS
The
.Fl -stats
line reports bytes read, records fully written, elapsed wall time, throughput,
worker count, process CPU time and peak resident memory.
CPU time covers all threads during processing; peak RSS covers the process
lifetime.
Text output uses IEC byte units and marks processing failures as
.Ql incomplete .
Unavailable resource measurements appear as
.Ql n/a .
.Pp
JSON statistics have these keys:
.Bl -tag -width peak_rss_bytes -compact
.It Cm stats_version
The integer 1, or 2 with
.Fl -stats-version Cm 2 .
.It Cm bytes_read
Bytes returned by input reads, including read-ahead.
.It Cm records
Records completely written to standard output.
.It Cm workers
Worker count used for processing.
.It Cm wall_us
Elapsed processing time in microseconds.
.It Cm complete
Whether processing finished without an input or processing error.
.It Cm cpu_user_us , cpu_system_us
User and system CPU time in microseconds, when available.
.It Cm peak_rss_bytes
Process peak resident memory in bytes, when available and positive.
.It Cm stop_reason
Why processing ended, with
.Fl -stats-version Cm 2 :
.Cm eof
after every input reached end of file,
.Cm limit
when
.Fl -limit
records were written,
.Cm duration
when the
.Fl -duration-ms
deadline passed,
.Cm output_closed
when the reader of standard output closed it,
.Cm cancelled
after an external stop request, or
.Cm error
after a failure.
Any value other than
.Cm eof
means the inputs may hold more records.
Version 2 sets
.Cm stats_version
to 2 and ends the text line with
.Ql , stop: Ar reason .
.El
.Pp
Statistics follow diagnostics on standard error and keep the processing exit
status.
An assertion failure reports
.Cm complete=true ;
the exit status carries the assertion result.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width NO_COLOR
.It Ev NO_COLOR
Its presence, including an empty value, disables automatic text coloring.
.It Ev TERM
The value
.Cm dumb
disables automatic text coloring.
.El
.Sh FILES
Paths below are relative to the installation prefix, by default
.Pa /usr/local .
Packages set their own documentation and data directories.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa share/doc/lumtail/
Guide, examples, sandbox documentation and license notices.
.It Pa share/lumtail/contracts/cli/
Published CLI API 1 definitions, extensions, schemas and compatibility
consumers.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Successful processing, including zero matches, a requested record/time limit,
or downstream pipe closure with no assertion.
.It 1
Input, output, sandbox or processing failure; clipped agent output with
.Fl -fail-on-truncation ;
or an assertion made inconclusive by downstream closure.
Records written before the error are complete.
.It 2
Invalid arguments, an unknown module or invalid rules/configuration.
.It 3
An unsatisfied
.Fl -expect-none
or
.Fl -expect-some
assertion, with no higher-priority processing error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Read warnings and errors from two files, preserving file order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --min-level warn app.log worker.log
.Ed
.Pp
Select API errors in production; all three conditions must match:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --errors-only --where service=api --where env=prod app.log
.Ed
.Pp
Export HTTP 5xx records with fields and source locations:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --output json \e
  --filter 'status>=500 AND status<600' access.log
.Ed
.Pp
Keep indented stack traces and print the header's location:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --multiline indent --min-level error \e
  --template '{file}:{line} [{level}] {message}' app.log
.Ed
.Pp
Read a live producer and emit records as they become available:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
journalctl -f -o json | lumtail --line-buffered --min-level error
.Ed
.Pp
Inspect authentication failures in a rotating file for five seconds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --follow --duration-ms 5000 --auth-failures \e
  --preset auth auth.log
.Ed
.Pp
Query at most 20 records with selected context:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --agent --limit 20 --agent-fields service,user,src_ip app.log
.Ed
.Pp
Check for errors using the exit status, discarding record output:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --expect-none --errors-only app.log > /dev/null
.Ed
.Pp
Status 0 means zero selected records were observed; status 3 means at least one.
Use a shell's
.Ql set -o pipefail
when producer failures must propagate through a pipeline.
.Pp
Load a custom format and inspect its detection priority:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail --rules application-rules.json --list-modules
lumtail --rules application-rules.json --module application app.log
.Ed
.Pp
Read a filename beginning with a hyphen:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
lumtail -- -application.log
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr lumtail-rules 5 ,
.Xr lumtail-agent 7 ,
.Xr lumtail-formats 7
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Log records are untrusted input;
.Sx OUTPUT
describes how each mode escapes them.
Add
.Fl -require-sandbox
when reading logs written by other users or hosts.
The review rules for untrusted log content are in
.Pa share/doc/lumtail/docs/safety.md .
.Sh LIMITS
A record/time limit or downstream closure can leave input unread.
Assertions describe complete records observed during that run; a zero-match
result applies to that inspected input window.
There is no global marker for an early stop at
.Fl -limit .
.Pp
The duration bounds input collection with polling granularity.
Startup, rule loading, an in-progress callback and blocking filesystem/output
operations add to total runtime.
Queued processing failures still cause failure after a requested early stop.
.Pp
Writes to the old file after its final rotation EOF can be missed.
Truncation followed by regrowth past the previous offset between polls can
also go undetected.
.Pp
Indentation grouping uses leading whitespace; unindented stack-trace lines
start separate records.
On cgroup v1 hosts, set workers explicitly to match container CPU quotas.
Ctrl-C terminates the process before its final statistics line.
